System Documentation: Automated Apache Tika Server Setup in WSL
1. Objective
This document outlines the procedure for configuring a persistent Apache Tika server on a WSL2 instance running Ubuntu 24.04. The final state is a Tika server that starts automatically on WSL launch, managed by the systemd init system for maximum reliability.
2. Prerequisites
A WSL2 instance with Ubuntu 24.04 (or similar modern Debian/Ubuntu).
The user has sudo (administrator) privileges.
docker.io has been installed via sudo apt-get install docker.io.
3. Configuration Steps
Step 3.1: Enable systemd in WSL
The systemd init system is required to manage services at boot. It is not enabled by default in WSL.
Create the WSL configuration file to enable systemd on boot. This command overwrites or creates the file.
sudo tee /etc/wsl.conf <<EOF
[boot]
systemd = true
EOF
Use code with caution.
Bash
Fully shut down WSL for the change to take effect. This command must be run from PowerShell or CMD.
wsl --shutdown
Use code with caution.
Powershell
Step 3.2: Configure and Enable the Docker Service
Ensure the Docker daemon is configured correctly and enabled to start on boot. A common issue is a conflict between Docker's daemon.json file and the systemd service configuration.
Fix daemon.json configuration. The hosts key conflicts with systemd. This command overwrites /etc/docker/daemon.json, removing the hosts key while preserving other settings.
sudo tee /etc/docker/daemon.json <<EOF
{
  "iptables": false,
  "log-driver": "json-file",
  "log-opts": {
    "max-size": "10m",
    "max-file": "3"
  }
}
EOF
Use code with caution.
Bash
Reload the systemd configuration to recognize the changes.
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
Use code with caution.
Bash
Enable the Docker service to ensure it starts automatically on every WSL launch.
sudo systemctl enable docker.service
Use code with caution.
Bash
Step 3.3: Create the Tika systemd Service
Instead of relying on Docker's --restart flag, we create a dedicated systemd service to manage the Tika container. This provides superior dependency management and ensures Tika only starts after Docker is fully running.
Create a new service file for Tika using a text editor like nano.
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/tika.service
Use code with caution.
Bash
Add the following service definition to the file. This configuration defines how to start, stop, and manage the Tika container.
[Unit]
Description=Apache Tika Server Container
# This service requires Docker to be running and starts after it.
Requires=docker.service
After=docker.service

[Service]
Restart=always
# Before starting, ensure no old container with the same name exists.
# The '-' prefix means the command can fail without stopping the service.
ExecStartPre=-/usr/bin/docker stop tika-server
ExecStartPre=-/usr/bin/docker rm tika-server
# The main command to start the Tika container.
# Note the absence of the '-d' (detach) flag; systemd handles backgrounding.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/docker run --name tika-server -p 9998:9998 --memory=2g --cpus=1 apache/tika:latest-full
# How to gracefully stop the container.
ExecStop=/usr/bin/docker stop tika-server

[Install]
# Hooks the service into the standard boot-up target.
WantedBy=multi-user.target
Use code with caution.
Ini
Save the file and exit the editor (Ctrl+X, Y, Enter).
Enable the new Tika service so it will start on boot.
sudo systemctl enable tika.service
Use code with caution.
Bash
4. Verification and Usage
Start the services for the current session (this happens automatically on future launches).
sudo systemctl start docker.service
sudo systemctl start tika.service
Use code with caution.
Bash
Verify the services are running and enabled.
# Check Docker status
sudo systemctl status docker.service

# Check Tika status
sudo systemctl status tika.service
Use code with caution.
Bash
Both should show active (running) in green text.
Confirm the Tika container is running using the docker command.
docker ps
Use code with caution.
Bash
You should see the tika-server container listed.
Final Test. Perform another wsl --shutdown from PowerShell, then relaunch your WSL instance. Run docker ps immediately to confirm the tika-server container started automatically.
The Tika server is now fully automated and available at http://localhost:9998 on your local machine.
